import json

upsc = []

# ==================================================================
# CHAPTER: INDIAN PARLIAMENTARY GROUP (Laxmikanth, Ch.25) - standalone chapter
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about the Indian Parliamentary Group (IPG) is NOT correct?","o":["The IPG was established by a statute of Parliament in 1952, making it a statutory body","The IPG was formed in 1949 in pursuance of a motion adopted by the Constituent Assembly (Legislative) on August 16, 1948","The IPG is an autonomous body whose membership is open to all members of Parliament","Former members of Parliament can become associate members of the IPG, though with limited rights"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect — the IPG is an AUTONOMOUS body, not one created by a parliamentary statute; it was formed in 1949 pursuant to a motion adopted by the Constituent Assembly (Legislative) on 16 August 1948. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about the office-bearers of the Indian Parliamentary Group (IPG):\n1. The Speaker of the Lok Sabha is the ex officio President of the IPG.\n2. The Deputy Speaker of the Lok Sabha and the Deputy Chairman of the Rajya Sabha are the ex officio vice-presidents of the IPG.\n3. The Secretary-General of the Rajya Sabha acts as the ex officio Secretary-General of the IPG.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect — it is the Secretary-General of the LOK SABHA (not the Rajya Sabha) who acts as the ex officio Secretary-General of the IPG. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"The Indian Parliamentary Group (IPG) functions as the National Group of which international organisation, and also as the India Branch of which other body?","o":["National Group of the Inter-Parliamentary Union (IPU); India Branch of the Commonwealth Parliamentary Association (CPA)","National Group of the Commonwealth Parliamentary Association (CPA); India Branch of the Inter-Parliamentary Union (IPU)","National Group of the United Nations; India Branch of the Commonwealth Parliamentary Association","National Group of the Inter-Parliamentary Union; India Branch of the South Asian Association for Regional Cooperation"],"a":0,"e":"The IPG acts both as the National Group of the Inter-Parliamentary Union (IPU) and as the India Branch of the Commonwealth Parliamentary Association (CPA) — its dual role in maintaining India's inter-parliamentary links."})

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about the Inter-Parliamentary Union (IPU), per the Indian Parliamentary Group's account, is NOT correct?","o":["The IPU is a body restricted exclusively to Commonwealth countries and their legislatures","The IPU is an international organisation of the parliaments of sovereign states, consisting of 179 parliaments of sovereign nations","The IPU's aim is to work for peace and cooperation among peoples and for the firm establishment of representative institutions","The IPU fosters contacts, coordination and exchange of experience among parliaments and parliamentarians of member countries"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect — the IPU is not restricted to Commonwealth countries; it is a broader international organisation of parliaments of sovereign states worldwide (179 member parliaments). It is the CPA, not the IPU, that is specifically a Commonwealth-focused body. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about the Commonwealth Parliamentary Association (CPA) is NOT correct?","o":["The CPA is an association of about 17,000 Parliamentarians and Parliamentary staff spread over 180 National, State, Provincial and Territorial Parliaments and Legislatures in 53 Commonwealth countries","The CPA's mission is to promote the advancement of parliamentary democracy by enhancing knowledge and understanding of democratic governance","The IPG functions as the main branch of the CPA in India","CPA membership entitles IPG members to receive 'The Parliamentarian' and the newsletter 'First Reading' only on payment of an additional annual subscription"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect — per the text, all members of the IPG are entitled to receive 'The Parliamentarian' (quarterly) and 'First Reading' (every second month) FREE OF CHARGE, not on payment of an additional subscription. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about the objectives and functions of the Indian Parliamentary Group (IPG):\n1. One of its stated aims is to promote personal contact between members of the Parliament of India.\n2. It arranges seminars, discussions and orientation courses, and brings out publications for disseminating information to its members.\n3. It arranges visits to foreign countries to develop contacts with members of other parliaments.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"a":0,"e":"All three statements are correct and match the stated aims and objectives of the IPG as described in the text: promoting personal contact among Indian MPs, organising seminars/publications, and arranging visits abroad."})

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about eligibility for inclusion in Indian Parliamentary delegations to foreign countries, per the IPG's rules, is correct?","o":["Only members of Parliament who have been members of the IPG for at least six months' standing at the time of composition of the delegation may be included","Any member of Parliament, regardless of IPG membership duration, may be included in such delegations","Only associate members of the IPG (former MPs) are eligible for inclusion in such delegations","Membership of the IPG is not a relevant criterion for inclusion in Indian Parliamentary delegations abroad"],"a":0,"e":"The text explicitly states that only those members of Parliament who are members of the IPG of at least six months' standing at the time of the composition of the delegation may be included in Indian Parliamentary delegations to foreign countries."})

upsc.append({"q":"Assertion (A): The Indian Parliamentary Group instituted an award for 'Outstanding Parliamentarian' in 1995, to be given annually.\nReason (R): A committee of five persons, constituted by the Speaker of the Lok Sabha, invites and finalises the nomination for this award.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above Assertion (A) and Reason (R)?","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true and R explains A — the text states the Outstanding Parliamentarian award was instituted in 1995 as a decision of the IPG, and explains the selection mechanism: a five-person committee constituted by the Speaker of the Lok Sabha invites and finalises nominations."})

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about Parliamentary Friendship Groups (PFGs) is NOT correct?","o":["PFGs are constituted independently of the IPG, with their management and control vesting directly in the Ministry of External Affairs","Each PFG consists of sitting members of Parliament, drawn from both the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha","The Speaker of the Lok Sabha appoints the President of each Parliamentary Friendship Group","PFG meetings are held within the Parliament House complex, typically on the sidelines of visiting foreign parliamentary delegations"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the actual arrangement — PFGs are constituted BY the IPG (to encourage bilateral relations with other countries), and the management and control of all their affairs vests in the IPG itself, not the Ministry of External Affairs. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about the aims and objectives of Parliamentary Friendship Groups (PFGs), per the Indian Parliamentary Group's charter:\n1. To maintain political, economic, social and cultural contact between the two countries concerned.\n2. To create favourable conditions for continuous development of inter-Parliamentary contacts, including talks, mutual exchanges and co-operation.\n3. To assist in exchanges of information and experiences on issues related to Parliamentary activities.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"a":0,"e":"All three statements are correct and reflect the stated aims of Parliamentary Friendship Groups: bilateral political/economic/social/cultural contact, development of inter-parliamentary contacts, and exchange of information/experience."})

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about the IPG Newsletter is correct?","o":["It is brought out every quarter and sent regularly to all members of the IPG, including associate members","It is published monthly and sent only to full (non-associate) members of the IPG","It is published by the CPA Secretariat in London rather than by the IPG itself","It was discontinued after the institution of the Outstanding Parliamentarian award in 1995"],"a":0,"e":"The text states an uninterrupted flow of information to members is maintained through the IPG Newsletter, which is brought out every quarter and sent regularly to all members of the IPG, including associate members."})

